  "query_preview": "..An upper low currently located over northern Baja this \nmorning. Out ahead of the upper low, cloud cover was increasing \nacross southwest Arizona earlier this morning associated with \nisolated light showers. Heating in conjunction with the upper low \nnudging closer will support isolated afternoon showers and perhaps a \nfew thunderstorms mainly with precipitation amounting to generally \nto around a tenth of an inch.\n\nTomorrow we'll see a bit more dynamical influence from the upper low \nas it morphs into a shortwave feature and ejects across southeast \nArizona into New Mexico. Combined with daytime heating, the wave \nwill support isolated to scattered storms from the Tucson Metro \neastward. Can't rule out a few stronger storms capable of brief \nheavy rain, gusty winds, and small hail.\n\nAfter lingering shower and storm chances mainly east of Tucson and \nin the White Mountains on Tuesday, we look to the next storm system \nwhich is diving down out of the Pacific Northwest. Right now the EOF \ncontinues to highlight minor timing differences from run to run, but \nfor now it looks like we'll see scattered showers and thunderstorms \nin the Wednesday through Thursday timeframe. Upon collaboration with \nthe Weather Prediction Center (WPC) we have introduced a marginal \nrisk for the excessive rainfall on Wednesday (Day 4) and a slight \nrisk for Thursday (Day 5) across northern portions of our forecast \narea including Pinal, Graham, and Greenlee counties. Spatially, this \nequates to a 5-15% chance for exceeding flash flood guidance within \n25 miles of a point.\n\nOtherwise, temperatures through the forecast period will generally \nbe slightly below normal, though near normal temperatures will occur \naround Tuesday and Wednesday.",
